47e50edb37cca4b72cae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-149610" :question/id #uuid "998f0865-3bec-5b9e-8f62-a7bf6559d032" :question/math-academy-id "q-149610" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y.$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)\\ge 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038247/assets/c35c98904d2a0d0d6d49c582cd9f9dba9d96e91ae0da5deb2d329f38f77532e3.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038247/assets/9340582eaad37c4ce1e0c8cbcdce48faec1e36c3c05adbedd61aa2de7168fd2f.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-{x}^{2}$ | $y$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n  \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038247/assets/17dd9e10234b6a69123241a882e267d95d505d1944d7c31ef563f9ac06b990af.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-149610/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "490982ac-6197-58eb-8d94-2ef28d659d50"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-149610"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-317169/field-1/ccfe516fb6b2e6a04cc571ee2ff57ef9ea3f598a00c4bac0e1422b5f957652bc" :answ