} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038247/assets/5fd85410238231152b6f905921f594af15293d6efc54b0b1ce558131a05bf6ee.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038247/assets/e95107f16eb80a06cd172c30ec78992838f974a91f9f2fa17b7c8ac77125c438.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | ${x}^{3}$ | ${y}^{5}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038247/assets/36e292eea1cf0be1527b2a357f1939e4c95f0202aa0ce5b91e9079da493f2155.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-317169/field-1" "q-317169/field-2" "q-317169/field-3" "q-317169/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "798800a5-255a-5738-91d2-21c2a78e4fb5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-317169"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-317214/field-1/4e1443bd31b83d9674b14c2b39fdc69b7291d3991d33de8027815afac4720949" :answer/id #uuid "2c4a6e66-515b-5ffa-8f0d-6d666a4d930e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "+" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-317214/field-